00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
So let's get started, shall we?

124
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Scout Master Ship in seven minutes

125
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Or less.

126
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
So there's this guy. he goes out, he buys this new electronic gizmo

127
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
and he brings it home and he tries to get it to work

128
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and it just won't work

129
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
And he spent a fair amount of money on it and he's getting a little chuffed.

130
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
So he finds the 800 number of the company,

131
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
he gets a representative on the phone

132
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,000
and he gives them a hard time because this gizmo just won't work

133
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And they say, well, let's go through the troubleshooting list here

134
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and he says, oh, it just doesn't work

135
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and I need to figure out how to make it work.

136
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
And so the first step in the troubleshooting list

137
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
is: did you put the batteries in it?

138
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
And then there's a little silence on the other end of the phone

139
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
and the guy says: well, I think I know why it doesn't work. now

140
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
We've all had one of those experiences

141
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
where we got something and we were pretty sure

142
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
we absolutely knew how it would work.

143
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
so we just took it right out of the package

144
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and started pressing buttons,

145
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
but found out that we actually didn't know.

146
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
So what did we do?

147
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
We read the manual. right,

148
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
We went and looked at the manual,

149
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
figured it out- usually something very simple,

150
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
just something we misunderstood.

151
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
and so we read the manual

152
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
and we figured out exactly how to make it work.

153
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
And I say that because sometimes,

154
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
when we're dealing with requirements

155
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
for merit badges or for ranks,

156
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
the way that we think that those requirements are fulfilled

157
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
does not really job with what's actually written

158
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
in the Boy Scout handbook.

159
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Now let me preface this by saying

160
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
I don't sign off many requirements.

161
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Requirements for ranks up to first class

162
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
are almost exclusively signed off by our older scouts.

163
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
I do sign the requirements

164
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
if I'm functioning as a merit badge counselor,

165
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
but not so much in my role as a scout master.

166
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
I need to train our older scouts

167
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,000
how to sign off requirements,

168
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,000
and when I do this,

169
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
I tell them to look for the verbs and modifiers

170
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
written in the requirements

171
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and pay attention to them.

172
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
I tell them to read the requirement carefully,

173
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
no matter how many times they think they've signed it off,

174
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:13,000
and make sure that they're actually doing what it says,

175
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
rather than just glossing it over

176
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
or just going on what they think they understand

177
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
and getting the wrong idea.

178
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
If you're signing off requirements for ranks or merit badges,

179
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
this is a pretty crucial thing.

180
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
It's so simple that we miss it.

181
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
I've been counseling camping merit badge now for,

182
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
oh gosh, 20 years or so,

183
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and I have learned to make myself go back

184
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and read the requirements,

185
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
because to get them right

186
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
means understanding exactly what they say.

187
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
So here's some examples

188
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
of what I mean by actually reading these requirements:

189
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
The requirement for the scout badge,

190
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
the joining badge,

191
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
requirement number seven says:

192
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
understand and agree to live by the scout oath or promise

193
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
the scout law, the motto and the slogan

194
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and the outdoor code.

195
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Understand and agree-

196
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
two very important words here.

197
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
That does not mean repeat from memory.

198
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
In some ways, this is a higher standard

199
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
than just memorizing it.

200
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
I mean, what does it mean to be thrifty?

201
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
What does it mean to be reverent?

202
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
What does the outdoor code mean?

203
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
How are you going to live by it?

204
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
So this is not just memorizing these things,

205
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
but understanding them

206
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and agreeing to live by them.

207
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Now I will bet that some of you, listening

208
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
when you get to this requirement,

209
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
expect scouts to have these things memorized.

210
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
But that's not the important part of the requirement.

211
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
If you're joining scouts,

212
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
you need to understand these things

213
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
and you need to agree to live by them.

214
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
You don't have to have them memorized at that point,

215
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
because that will come later on.

216
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
I think it's in the tenderfoot requirements

217
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
where it says: repeat from memory.

218
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
Here's another typical word that you find in requirements.

219
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Let's look at tenderfoot number 12B.

220
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
It says: show first aid for the following,

221
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
and then it lists a number of first aid scenarios.

222
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Now, show does not mean talk about.

223
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
it does not mean write down,

224
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
It means show.

225
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
So, to complete this requirement,

226
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
a scout's going to use his hands and a first aid kit

227
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and he's going to show what needs to happen

228
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
in the cases listed in that requirement.

229
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Another good word to look for is demonstrate.

230
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
In second class 1A.

231
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
it asks a scout to demonstrate how a compass works

232
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
and how to orient a map

233
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
and then to explain what map symbols mean.

234
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Demonstrate doesn't mean tell.

235
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
It means actively showing the skill,

236
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
Showing how it's accomplished,

237
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
using the actual tools required to accomplishment.

238
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Explain indicates that a scout

239
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
should not only know the information

240
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
but is also needs to know what it means.

241
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Those are other important verbs

242
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and modifiers to look for.

243
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
First class number 6 says identify

244
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
or show evidence of at least 10 kinds

245
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
of native plants found in your community.

246
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Identify means being able to recognize

247
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
or distinguish something,

248
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
and that's modified by show evidence of

249
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
indicating to me that the actual object

250
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
being identified may or may not be present.

251
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
It may not be the plant itself,

252
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
It may be a picture of it.

253
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
The modifier native is important too.

254
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
What does that mean

255
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And how does it modify the type of plant

256
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
used to fulfill the requirement?

257
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
Requirement 3 begins with:

258
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
make a written plan for an overnight track

259
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
and show how to get to your camping spot

260
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
using a topographical map or compass etc.

261
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Make a written plan

262
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
is not telling or demonstrating,

263
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
It's writing.

264
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
You don't see all that many requirements

265
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
that require book work or writing.

266
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
I mean thank goodness right.

267
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
But some of them are very specific

268
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
about producing a written plan.

269
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
When we read scout requirements

270
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
and take them at face value,

271
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
we'll better serve the intention

272
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
embedded in the scout requirement

273
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
and we'll be less likely to water them down

274
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
or to add to them.

275
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
Now we posted about this on the blog

276
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
a week or two ago

277
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and it's had some great comments on it.

278
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
One came from Walter Underwood.

279
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
He said one other thing:

280
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
if you read carefully, the requirements are written

281
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
to allow for disabilities.

282
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
It doesn't say: write a plan,

283
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Just make a written plan.

284
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Maybe you need to dictate that plan to somebody,

285
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
And that's an excellent point.

286
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
Yes, there are alternative requirements

287
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
for scouts who are differently able,

288
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
but if you look at the requirements,

289
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
they will be able to do a lot of them

290
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
if you just read them carefully.

291
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Now I did have one comment that I disagreed with,

292
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and that was well. when you're checking

293
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
scouts off on requirements

294
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
or when scouts are being checked off on requirements,

295
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
other scouts shouldn't be able to listen in on that,

296
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
because then they'll just pair it what they heard

297
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
And you know what.

298
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
I don't agree with that

299
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
because I really don't care how a scout learns,

300
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
so long as he gets it,

301
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
And I'll bet he learned it pretty well

302
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
if he sees or hears the right skill

303
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
or information repeated a bunch of times,

304
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
watching other scouts repeat that same skill

305
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
or giving the same answer.

306
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
Years ago I was a counselor

307
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:30,000
for rifle-shooting merit badge at our summer camp

308
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and counseled 20 to 30 scouts every week

309
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
And we had what I called a test on Friday

310
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
to certify some of the requirements for the badge.

311
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,000
When you're dealing with 30 guys every week,

312
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
you have to be creative in some of the evaluations.

313
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
The last thing I wanted to do

314
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
was give them a worksheet or something like that

315
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and have them fill it out and then have me grade it

316
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
and then hand it back to them

317
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
and then have them hand it back to me.

318
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
And you know that's schoolwork.

319
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
We don't do schoolwork in scouts.

320
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
So this is the way that I used to handle these groups.

321
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
When Friday rolled around,

322
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
they would come down to the rifle range.

323
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Each of them would have a piece of paper

324
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and something to write with

325
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and I would start asking them questions

326
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
or showing them something

327
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
that they had to name

328
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
and write their answer on a piece of paper,

329
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
And I guess there was maybe 20 questions

330
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
or 20 things that I would show them

331
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and they would write them down on the paper

332
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
and when we were finished doing that,

333
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
they would exchange that paper with another scout.

334
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Let's say, number one was the three rules

335
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
for safe firearms handling,

336
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
And we would go over that

337
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
point by point

338
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
and make sure that everybody had it right.

339
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:46,000
if they didn't have it right,

340
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
Well, you know, circle the number there

341
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and we'll move on.

342
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
Now, once we went through all the questions,

343
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:55,000
the papers were given back to the original owners

344
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and anyone who had answered them

345
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
all correctly was done.

346
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
I asked for their paper.

347
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
I recorded the fact that they knew

348
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
what they were talking about

349
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
and they had finished that part of the merit page.

350
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Scouts who had a wrong answer

351
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
stayed right there

352
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and I talked to each one individually,

353
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
within the earshot

354
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
of everybody else,

355
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and I would ask them to give me the right answers

356
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Now. they had just heard the right answer.

357
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
They had just heard the right answer

358
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
because we had just gone over them

359
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
with the whole group.

360
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
If they couldn't remember the right answer,

361
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
I pointed them to a copy of the merit page book

362
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
and asked them to look up the right answer

363
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and then I moved on to the next scout.

364
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Pretty soon the whole group had figured out,

365
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
they could talk to each other

366
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
and help each other get all the answers right.

367
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Needless to say, the scouts

368
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
who hadn't bothered to know anything,

369
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
who hadn't- quote unquote- studied

370
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
for the test,

371
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
had heard the correct answers

372
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
and repeated several times by now

373
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
and they had to sit down with me

374
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
individually and they had to give me the correct answer

375
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
and I'm confident

376
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
that when they all left the rifle range

377
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
they knew their stuff and we had

378
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
actually a pretty good time getting there.

379
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
It doesn't matter to me how they learn things.

380
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
if they hear it repeated all the time, it's fine.

381
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
I understand that school wants to build

382
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
study habits and things like that

383
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
and you know that's all good.

384
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
but we're not school.

385
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
We don't use school methods of instructing

386
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
or testing. We use scouting methods.

387
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
How do scouts learn?

388
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Any way they can, and I can't think of

389
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
any wrong way

390
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
to learn the right things.

