418
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
I. had an email this week from

419
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Brad in New Hampshire.

420
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
He says our troop is getting ready

421
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
to realign patrols. I've read the patrol

422
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
method in practice on your website.

423
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
If boys choose their own patrols,

424
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
what happens

425
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
to several scouts in our troop

426
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
who are not necessarily liked

427
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
by all the scouts? Do they wind up without

428
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
a patrol? If they choose

429
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
a patrol, can the patrol

430
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
of scouts refuse them?

431
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
Well, Brad, thanks for getting in touch

432
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
and thanks for this question.

433
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
Just to catch everybody up, we talk

434
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
about scouts choosing their own patrols,

435
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
The patrol leaders council sitting down,

436
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
figuring things out, letting them

437
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
sort it out.

438
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
There is always the kind of danger

439
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
of that

440
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
scenario that we've seen again and again

441
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
of like picking teams

442
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
or something and

443
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
the athletic guy is kind of

444
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
the last guy picked

445
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
and nobody wants to create

446
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
anything like that. If your troop doesn't

447
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
already have a kind of accepting, friendly

448
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
culture, you need to work

449
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
on creating that a little bit. You may

450
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
ask for a few minutes of time

451
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
before the scouts are going to do all this

452
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
and talk to the whole troop

453
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
and ask a scout to read

454
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
from the handbook the definition

455
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
of what it says in the scout law

456
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
when we pledge ourselves to be friendly.

457
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
It says: a scout is friendly,

458
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
a scout's a friend to all,

459
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
he's a brother to all scouts.

460
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
he offers his friendship to people of all races,

461
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
religions and nations

462
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
and respects them, even if their beliefs

463
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and customs are different from his own.

464
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
And then start asking the boys

465
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
some questions about this.

466
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
What does being friendly mean?

467
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
How do you treat people that you don't

468
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
really like? What

469
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
things do people do that makes you

470
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
dislike them?

471
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
What does it feel like when other people

472
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
aren't friendly to you?

473
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
Why do you suppose being friendly is

474
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
a point of the scout law?

475
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
In what way does our troop

476
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
best demonstrate being friendly?

477
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
In what ways could we do a better job

478
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
of being friendly?

479
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
And then, you know, maybe tell a story about a time

480
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
you felt left out personally,

481
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
when nobody chose you for their

482
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
team, or something like that,

483
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
and encourage your scouts

484
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
to think about the choices

485
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
they're about to make. Tell them that

486
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
scouts is about

487
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
working with every other scout

488
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
and finding a way to be their friend,

489
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
even if you don't like them all that much.

490
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
What if everybody worked

491
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
very hard to do this? What if everybody

492
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
tried to find a way to be a friend

493
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
to everyone they came in contact with?

494
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
What would our world look like then?

495
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Maybe use this thought:

496
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
Imagine we're out camping and a fellow

497
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
scout is injured. Well, what would you do?

498
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
That's right. You'd come to his aid and help him.

499
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
What if he's someone you don't particularly like,

500
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
or someone who doesn't particularly

501
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
like you? You know what I mean.

502
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
We all work hard to get along, but sometimes

503
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
we fall short. If you were hurt,

504
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
you'd be looking for help and you'd accept

505
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
help from just about anyone, wouldn't you?

506
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
If you saw somebody who was hurt,

507
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
you'd offer them help, right.

508
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
You wouldn't walk away from them just because

509
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
they acted or differently

510
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
or dressed differently or looked differently

511
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
than you did. right. In the same way,

512
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
you'd freely offer your help to anyone

513
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
injured physically. like that,

514
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
it's just as important

515
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
to freely offer your friendship.

516
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
Sometimes it's hard to do because

517
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
other people can be difficult to like.

518
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
You can be difficult to like,

519
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
I can be difficult to like.

520
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
It's true of everybody sometimes.

521
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
One of the things that marks a good

522
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
scout is being friendly,

523
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
kind, helpful and loyal

524
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
to others. We ought to make sure

525
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
that it starts here. whenever

526
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
we're together with our fellow scouts,

527
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
Look around this room.

528
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
Every single scout in this room

529
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
is your friend. They'll be there for you

530
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
and you'll be there for them.

531
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
You don't have to like everything about them

532
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
